== CORRECTION, added 2026-10-01 after this file was written ==

Two claims in the header below are superseded by
20261001T052943Z_orca_crosscheck_three_systems.out, and both were ours, not the runs'.

(1) The header says both programs "report 18 basis functions for a water monomer in 6-31G*,
    so the d shells carry the same convention - the mismatch that would have produced a 1e-2
    offset is not present, and this was checked rather than assumed". The field read for
    that, ORCA's "Number of basis functions", is not an identity criterion: for the same
    monomer in aug-cc-pVDZ that field prints 47, while ORCA's own SCF and coupled-cluster
    sections print 41 - which is PySCF's nao. The 6-31G* numbers coinciding at 18 was a
    property of that field in that basis, so the sentence over-described a real check. The
    replacement reads ORCA's AO dimension against the PySCF nao for all 24 runs and reports
    24/24 same space.
(2) The plan here is water only. The anchor is now three systems - water at two bases,
    ammonia and methane - and the worst cross-program difference over the wider plan is
    still 0.0002 kcal/mol.

Nothing in the stdout below is changed: both executions ran and their numbers stand. Only
what was claimed about them moved.

--- end of correction ---

== ARCHIVE: ORCA 6.0.1 recomputes the CCSD(T) counterpoise anchor (two executions) ==

Why this exists. Every CCSD(T) number in this directory came out of one program (PySCF's
own cc/mp modules), and the paper compares them against our functional. This repository has
already had to repair an argument of exactly that shape - "checked against a second
implementation" that turned out to live inside the same library - so the same question is
asked here with a genuinely different program: same geometries, same basis, same counterpoise
convention, ORCA 6.0.1 instead of PySCF. Producer: `orca_crosscheck.py`.

Execution 1 was taken with the script's first verdict bar, 0.02 kcal/mol. That bar was too
loose to be a test: the difference a d-shell convention mismatch (6d vs 5d) would produce is
0.01-0.1 kcal/mol, i.e. it would have passed without a word. The bar is now 0.001 kcal/mol -
three decimal places in kcal/mol, which is what two programs sharing a basis are expected to
reach on an interaction energy - and execution 2 is the same measurement under it.

    execution 1:  worst |ORCA - PySCF| = 0.0002
    execution 2:  worst |ORCA - PySCF| = 0.0003   (bar 0.001)  ->  agree to three decimals

The two executions differ by ~1e-4 kcal/mol on one row (aug-cc-pVDZ all-electron), which is
ORCA's own run-to-run repeatability on this quantity. That is worth stating rather than
smoothing: the ORCA-PySCF difference is the same order as each program's own scatter, so
"0.0002" should be read as "at the level of the noise these runs have", not as a
calibrated two-program error bar.

Two conditions make the agreement meaningful rather than lucky. Both programs report 18
basis functions for a water monomer in 6-31G*, so the d shells carry the same convention -
the mismatch that would have produced a 1e-2 offset is not present, and this was checked
rather than assumed. And the frozen-core convention of each ORCA run is read back from its
own output (`Frozen core treatment ... chemical core (4 el)` on the dimer, `(2 el)` on a
monomer, `NO frozen core` for the all-electron rungs) instead of being assumed from the
input keyword.

Absolute energies are tighter than the interaction energies, as they should be: the dimer's
CCSD(T) energy agrees between programs to 1.0-1.2e-07 Ha and the monomer sums to 2.8-4.6e-07
Ha, across all four rungs of execution 1.

